# Fasha

Fasha is a web platform for finding volunteering opportunities in Rwanda, proposing your own
community initiatives, and building a record of service that someone else can verify.

I built it for my capstone project, which looks at why volunteering in Rwanda is harder to
access, join and report than it needs to be. The research found three problems, and the
platform is organised around them.

The first is discovery. Opportunities are shared across social media pages, word of mouth and
individual NGO websites, so there is no single place to look. If you are not already inside
someone's network, you tend not to hear about anything. Fasha puts everything in one feed and
ranks it by how well it fits the skills and interests you have listed.

The second is who gets to start things. Umuganda and most organised volunteering are planned
from the top down, which leaves people with few structured ways to act on needs they have
noticed themselves. On Fasha any volunteer can propose an initiative. It is reviewed before it
goes public, but the idea can come from anyone.

The third is proof. Attendance is usually recorded on paper registers that are slow to
consolidate and easy to lose, so volunteers end up with no reliable record of what they have
done. Fasha records check in and check out against a code that changes every 30 seconds, lets
organisers review virtual work, and credits approved hours to a portfolio page the volunteer
can share with an employer or a scholarship panel.

**Repository:** https://github.com/lilalajemima/fasha
**Author:** Lilala Runiga Jemima, BSc Software Engineering, African Leadership University
**Supervisor:** Pelin Mutanguha

At this stage the frontend is complete and runs on a local mock data layer, and the database
schema is written. The API endpoints are the next sprint.

---

## Tools used

| Layer | Choice | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Frontend | React 19, TypeScript, Vite | Volunteers test this on their own phones, so it had to be a browser app with no install step |
| Routing | React Router 7 | Nested layout routes let both workspaces share one shell |
| Styling | Plain CSS with custom properties | No framework weight, and one file maps the Figma design to the code |
| Backend | Node.js, Express, TypeScript | A single modular monolith, as set out in the proposal |
| ORM | Prisma | Typed queries, and migrations I can version and replay |
| Database | PostgreSQL via Supabase | Native array columns, which is how matching compares skill and interest tags |
| Auth | bcrypt, JSON Web Tokens | Hashed passwords and stateless protected routes |
| Verification | otplib, qrcode | The event code is a time based one time password, so a photographed code expires |
| Fonts | Fugaz One, Work Sans | Display and body faces from the design system |

---

## Project structure

```
fasha/
├── frontend/
│   ├── public/                 favicon and icon sprite
│   ├── index.html
│   ├── vite.config.ts
│   └── src/
│       ├── styles/
│       │   ├── tokens.css      every colour, space and radius, taken from Figma
│       │   └── global.css      layout, buttons, forms, cards, tables, breakpoints
│       ├── components/
│       │   ├── ui.tsx          Button, Card, Tag, StatusBadge, StatTile, Field, Tabs, FileDrop
│       │   └── ImigongoBand.tsx
│       ├── auth/
│       │   ├── AuthContext.tsx session state, on localStorage until JWT lands
│       │   └── RequireAuth.tsx the role guard that keeps the two account types apart
│       ├── layouts/
│       │   ├── Shell.tsx       sidebar on desktop, tab bar on phone
│       │   ├── VolunteerShell.tsx
│       │   └── OrgShell.tsx
│       ├── mocks/              types.ts, data.ts, api.ts
│       ├── pages/
│       │   ├── auth/           SignUp, SignIn, AuthPanel
│       │   ├── volunteer/      Discover, OpportunityDetail, MyActivity, Profile,
│       │   │                   Portfolio, MyInitiatives, CreateOpportunity, CheckIn
│       │   └── org/            Overview, Opportunities, ReviewWorkspace, Attendance,
│       │                       OpportunityTable
│       ├── App.tsx             all routes, grouped by role guard
│       └── main.tsx
└── backend/
    ├── prisma/schema.prisma    the data model: 8 models and 7 enums
    └── prisma.config.ts        Prisma configuration
```

Every screen reads its data through `frontend/src/mocks/api.ts`. That was deliberate. Each
function in that file becomes a `fetch` call to the API later, and no screen component has to
change when that happens.

---

## Setting up the environment and the project

### What you need first

- Node.js 20 and npm. Check with `node -v`.
- Git.
- A PostgreSQL database. A free Supabase project is quickest, but a local Postgres 16 install
  works just as well.

### Clone it

```bash
git clone https://github.com/lilalajemima/fasha.git
cd fasha
```

### Frontend

```bash
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
```

Vite prints a local address, usually `http://localhost:5173`.

The backend is not connected yet, so any email and password will sign you in. Pick Volunteer or
Organisation on the sign in card and you will land in that workspace. To switch, go back to
`/signin` and choose the other one, or run `localStorage.clear()` in the browser console and
reload.

The other commands:

```bash
npm run build     # type checks and builds into dist/
npm run lint      # ESLint
npm run preview   # serves the built bundle
```

Run `npm run build` before you commit. The dev server is forgiving about things like a missing
export, and the build is not, which is useful.

### Backend

```bash
cd backend
npm install
```

Create `backend/.env`:

```
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres"
JWT_SECRET="a long random string"
TOTP_WINDOW_SECONDS=30
PORT=4000
CLIENT_ORIGIN="http://localhost:5173"
```

There are two database URLs because Prisma needs a direct connection to run migrations, while
the running server uses the pooled one. `prisma.config.ts` reads `DIRECT_URL` for that reason.

Then set up the database:

```bash
npx prisma generate
npx prisma migrate dev --name init
npx prisma studio      # optional, opens a browser view of the tables
```

`.env` is in `.gitignore` and should stay out of the repository.

---

## Deployment plan

### Where it will run

| Part | Platform | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Web client | Vercel | Static build from `frontend/`, served from a CDN |
| API | Render | Node service built from `backend/` |
| Database | Supabase | Managed PostgreSQL, pooled for the app and direct for migrations |
| File storage | Supabase Storage | Resumes, event photos, deliverables |

I went with managed services rather than hosting anything myself. The pilot runs for a month
with 25 to 40 users, which the free tiers cover comfortably, and neither I nor the people
testing it should depend on a machine that has to stay switched on.

### Environments

| Environment | For | Database |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Local | Development | Local Postgres, or a separate Supabase project |
| Production | The Kimironko pilot | Supabase production project |

### Releasing

1. Merge the feature branch into `main` after review.
2. Run `npx prisma migrate deploy` against the production database. Migrations live in
   `backend/prisma/migrations/` and are applied in order, never by hand.
3. Render rebuilds and restarts the API. Vercel rebuilds and publishes the client.
4. Check it: `GET /health` returns 200, then sign in, load the feed and do one check in.
5. If something is wrong, roll back to the previous deployment on each platform. Migrations are
   written to be additive, so rolling back does not lose data.

### Configuration

No secret is committed. Each platform holds its own environment variables: `DATABASE_URL`,
`DIRECT_URL`, `JWT_SECRET`, `TOTP_WINDOW_SECONDS` and `CLIENT_ORIGIN` on Render, and
`VITE_API_URL` on Vercel.

### Thinking about the pilot

People will use this on their own phones, on mobile data, outdoors. That shaped a few
decisions: it is a browser app so there is nothing to install, the production bundle is kept
small, the colours are high contrast rather than pale, and the QR check in has a manual
fallback for the moments when the network drops.